# Method: djvu/hocr search text to locate pages, then PDF page images read by eye. Translations are the note author's own.
from _lib import note
TR = "*(English translation mine.)*"
FA = "https://archive.org/details/efath-moheb-elden-elkheeb"
MA = "https://archive.org/details/Almanar"

note("Events", "Al-Fath (Cairo), 1928 — Six Pieces on Kemal", {
    "type": "event", "when": "1928",
    "tags": ["event", "verified", "periodical", "contemporary", "hostile", "conflicted", "arabic-primary"]}, """
Continuing the round-7 card on *al-Fath* (Muhibb al-Din al-Khatib's Cairo weekly), this card reads further pages of the 1928 run (IA batch of nos. 1-125, [`efath-moheb-elden-elkheeb`]({fa})). Issue dates are from the mastheads (no. 79 = 12 Jan 1928; no. 86 = 1 Mar 1928; no. 96 = 17 May 1928; no. 102 = 28 Jun 1928; no. 112 = 6 Sep 1928; no. 119 = 25 Oct 1928). Printed page numbers; pdf page in brackets.

**1. "Memory of the abolition of the Caliphate" (no. 86, 1 Mar 1928, p. 569 [pdf 1369]), unsigned.** A "page from notes written on announcement of the abolition, 3 March 1924", printed four years on. A disappointed admirer: *"Mustafa Kemal, whose name we used to extol, for whom we prayed and whom we blessed, and from whom we hoped for the raising and honouring of Islam, acts thus towards Islam"* (مصطفى كمال الذي كنا نشيد باسمه وندعو له ونبارك عليه ونرجوه لرفعة الإسلام وإعزازه يفعل هكذا بالإسلام). The same page says the Anatolian soldier fought only out of Islamic motive and that the Indians and Afghans helped for the sake of the Caliphate Kemal then removed. Conflicted: the liberator's credit is conceded, the secularist condemned. {tr}

**2. "The religious fate of Turkey" (no. 96, 17 May 1928, pp. 721-723 [pdf 1521-1523]).** The paper credits it to "a great mujahid, the best-informed of Arabic writers on Turkey's affairs"; no name is printed (the description fits Shakib Arslan, but that is my inference only). It answers the claim that Turkey owes its revival to Kemal: *"it is not true, their claim that Mustafa Kemal is the one who freed Turkey; those who freed Turkey were a band of which Mustafa Kemal was one"* (فالذين حرروا تركيا هم عصابة كان مصطفى كمال واحداً منهم, p. 721). {tr}

**3. "The deluded" (no. 79, 12 Jan 1928, p. 452 [pdf 1252]).** Reprinted "from an article in *al-Akhbar* by one of the princes of eloquence, whose pen gives him away" (unnamed). After beating the Greeks Kemal *"thought himself to have become the man of the earth in length and breadth, and that all he says has become verses sent down from heaven"* (ظن نفسه أصبح رجل الأرض في الطول والعرض، وأن كل ما يقوله صار آيات منزلة من السماء). {tr} The piece also mocks his lecture on Rousseau.

**4. "The childhood of the Turkish leader, the false Robespierre" (no. 112, 6 Sep 1928, p. 172 [pdf 1778]), signed Muhammad Ali Gharib.** *"We do not much mind Mustafa Kemal's rancour against the Islamic religion and his hatred of it"* (ونحن لا نهتم كثيراً بحقد مصطفى كمال على الدين الإسلامي وكراهته له): he is an ordinary man, history will remember him for his childishness, not his genius. {tr}

**5. "Mustafa Kemal and Islam" (no. 102, 28 Jun 1928, pp. 17-18 [pdf 1617-1618]), signed Muhibb al-Din al-Khatib.** *"Mustafa Kemal has finished traversing the stages of the first leg of what he took upon himself, the demolition of the landmarks of Islam in Turkey"* (انتهى مصطفى كمال من طيّ مراحل الشوط الأول فيما أخذه على عاتقه من تقويض معالم الإسلام في تركيا). He lists the abolition of the sharia courts, the hat and prayer, and ends: "close your ranks". {tr}

**6. Jerusalem, no. 119 (25 Oct 1928, p. 301 [pdf 1897]): "Propaganda for the Turks in Palestine", signed al-Ri'awi al-Saghir (Jerusalem).** He reports a lecture "Renewal in Turkey and Islam" by Hasan Sidqi al-Dajani, who opened: *"What al-Ghazi Mustafa Kemal Pasha has done of brilliant works, which are a clear victory over time and a tremendous revolution and great development"* (إن ما قام به الغازي مصطفى كمال باشا من الأعمال الباهرة التي هي فوز مبين على الدهر، والتي هي انقلاب هائل وتطور عظيم), and claimed to have his news from the Turkish consul. The correspondent answers: *"this lecture is a stain of shame and filth with which the place where it was given was defiled"* (إن هذه المحاضرة لطخة عار وقذارة لوّث بها المكان الذي ألقيت فيه). Two voices, one admiring (Dajani, mediated by *al-Fath*) and one hostile. {tr}

**Caveats.** Items 1-3 are unsigned; item 2's author is not named. Quotes are the Arabic as read from page images (poor OCR, so no machine check).

## Sources
- *al-Fath* nos. 79, 86, 96, 102, 112, 119 (1928), pdf pages 1252, 1369, 1521-1523, 1617-1618, 1778, 1897 of the 1-125 batch: [archive.org]({fa}) · tier P
""".replace("{fa}", FA).replace("{tr}", TR))

note("Events", "Al-Manar 26–29 (1926–1928) — Rida on Kemal's Reforms", {
    "type": "event", "when": "1926–1928",
    "tags": ["event", "verified", "periodical", "contemporary", "hostile", "conflicted", "arabic-primary"]}, """
Later volumes of *al-Manar* (IA [`Almanar`]({ma}), `almanar26/28/29.pdf`), complementing the 1924 card. Unsigned editorial matter in Rida's own journal; I attribute it to Rida as editor and, where he writes "we" and cites his *Tafsir*, in his own voice.

**Vol. 26, part 7 (29 Jumada II 1344 / 14 Jan 1926), pp. 496-498 (pdf 382-384): the hat fatwa, headed "The Kemalists' flight from Islam".** Rida, answering a reader and a German writer, holds the hat permissible in itself but forbidden as a tool of the secularists: *"and the world will know the fate of the Turks from Mustafa Kemal's attempt to make them a new creation by these ways he has taken; we ask God to protect them from its evil outcome"* (وسيعلم العالم مصير الترك بمحاولة مصطفى كمال جعلهم خلقاً جديداً بهذه الطرق التي سلكها, p. 498). On p. 496 he says he believes Kemal "deliberately demolishes all the constituents of the Turkish people". {tr}

**Vol. 28, part 10 (30 Rajab 1346 / 23 Jan 1928), p. 785 (pdf 567), on the Afghan king's hat.** *"Sociologists of judgement and insight reckon that Mustafa Kemal's leap will be more dangerous to his people"* (يقدر أهل الرأي والبصيرة من علماء الاجتماع أن وثبة مصطفى كمال ستكون أشد خطراً على قومه): an old state shows such revival before it dies, like the flame of the wick before it goes out. {tr}

**Vol. 29, part 6 (30 Rabi II 1347 / 14 Oct 1928), pp. 456-470 (pdf 383-401): "The danger of the Kemalists' attack on Islam" (the Latin alphabet).** A first-person editorial. Section 3, "what Mustafa Kemal hopes to gain by his success", p. 466 (pdf 393), is the conflicted line: *"by my life, it is a great soul and a far-reaching ambition, though we Muslims abhor this new plan, and many of the wise think it unsound"* (ولعمري إنها لنفس كبيرة، وهمة بعيدة، وإن كنا نحن المسلمين نستنكر هذه الخطة الجديدة). He goes on to say Kemal aims to found a new nation and religion. {tr}

**Dry end.** Search of the OCR for vols. 30-35 (1929-35) found only Afghan-hat and passing items; not read. Vol. 27 p. 73 (pdf 68) compares Kemal to Mussolini but in a translated third-party article, so it is not recorded.

## Sources
- *al-Manar* 26(7) pp. 496-498; 28(10) p. 785; 29(6) pp. 456, 466: [archive.org]({ma}) · tier P
""".replace("{ma}", MA).replace("{tr}", TR))
